WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:06.600
Can you introduce yourself to the audience
of the Nexus Institute?

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:12.430
You wrote this great book, the first of a
series of four, on Lincoln.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:18.859
What are you - a journalist, a writer, a political
man?

00:00:18.859 --> 00:00:25.789
Well, I think the best way to describe myself
is through the experience that I brought to

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:29.310
bear in writing this book.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:34.579
I grew up in Chicago, Illinois.

00:00:34.579 --> 00:00:37.579
Illinois is Lincoln's state.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:45.920
It's in the middle of the United States, and
I have that perspective, that vantage point,

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:55.870
that I can bring to American society, culture
and politics.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.659
I became a journalist and have been a journalist
for many years.

00:01:00.659 --> 00:01:05.159
I worked for such publications as the New
Republic, the Washington Post

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.180
and the New Yorker.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:16.410
I've written many books about American politics,
and I served with president Clinton in the

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:17.410
White House.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:25.980
I've been active in campaigns and elections,
as a Democrat, and I'm a historian.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:34.360
Why is it that you got so involved in American
politics?

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:45.540
At the age of 12, I became entranced with
a presidential political campaign.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.660
That campaign was in 1960, and the candidate
was John F. Kennedy.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:57.600
When I grew up in Chicago there was a political
organization, a Democratic party organization.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.770
Every part of the city had what was called
a 'precinct captain', who was a member of

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.130
the Democratic Party.

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:15.860
That precinct captain recruited me as a boy,
to help him after school to get people to

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:16.860
vote.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:24.440
I would go around and speak to my neighbours
on election day, and say you would vote.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:32.890
And my reward was that my precinct captain
took me to the rally to see John F. Kennedy,

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.500
and that made an enormous impression on me.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:43.070
Shortly after that I was taken to Springfield,
Illinois, which is where Lincoln was from.

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:53.130
Springfield has many of the sites that Lincoln
was involved in: the State Capitol, his law

00:02:53.130 --> 00:03:00.560
office, the little town where he had lived
outside of Springfield as a young man.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:08.850
Shortly after that Martin Luther King came
to my city, and I saw Dr. King.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:18.130
And so I saw, even in the North, not the South,
the Civil Rights movement and its greatest

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:19.620
leader.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:27.100
All of these things combined to impress upon
me and awaken in me an interest in

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.640
American politics.

